---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../consts';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How we research and write"
  description="What AI Audio Report does and does not do: no hands-on testing, every price dated and sourced, named reviewers where a piece was checked, and corrections published openly."
>
  <h1 class="page-title">How we research and write</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The most useful thing on this page is the list of things this site will not do. That comes
    first.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site will not do</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>It will not claim hands-on testing.</strong> There is no test rig here and no shelf
    of plugin licences. You will not read “we tested”, “in our testing” or “after weeks of use”
    on this site, because none of it would be true. Where a comparison turns on how something
    sounds, the judgement belongs to a named third party and is linked so you can weigh the
    source yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>It will not invent people.</strong> No stock-photo reviewers, no fictional
    engineers with twenty years in the industry, no credentials that do not exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>It will not invent numbers.</strong> No estimated market sizes, no “studies show”
    without the study. Where a figure could not be verified, the article says so in those
    words. “We could not price this” and “the terms do not say” are publishable sentences
    here, and they appear fairly often, because the alternative is a confident guess.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>It will not publish an undated price.</strong> A price with no date attached is a
    price you have no reason to trust.
  </p>

  <h2>How a piece gets made</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      A topic is chosen because people are trying to make a specific decision and the existing
      results answer it badly, usually because they are stale or the vendor wrote them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Primary sources are fetched and read: terms of service, pricing pages, official policy
      documents, source repositories. A secondary source is used only where no primary one
      exists, and is named in the text when it is.
    </li>
    <li>
      The draft is then checked claim by claim against those sources. Anything that cannot be
      traced back to one is cut rather than hedged into something vaguer.
    </li>
    <li>Every source is listed at the end with the date it was read.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Treat that source list as the real product. The prose is a reading of those documents, and
    if the reading and the document disagree, the document wins and we want to hear about it.
  </p>

  <h2>Bylines and review</h2>
  <p>
    Articles carry an <strong>AI Audio Report editorial team</strong> byline. Where a piece has
    been read and checked line by line, the reviewer is named on it, and that name is
    <strong>Arnau Gran i Romero</strong>, who runs and edits the site. An article with no named
    reviewer has not had one yet. That distinction is on the page deliberately, because the
    alternative is letting you assume a level of oversight that was not there.
  </p>

  <h2>Keeping prices and terms current</h2>
  <p>
    Both move constantly in this niche. Every page carries the date its facts were verified and
    is scheduled for re-checking roughly every 30 days, and an updated page says when it was
    updated. A page whose dates have gone stale is a liability rather than an asset, and you
    should be able to spot one without reading it closely.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Errors get fixed, and the fix is noted on the page. Nothing is quietly edited out of
    existence. <a href={withBase('/contact/')}>Report a mistake</a>, especially a price that
    has moved. That is more useful than praise.
  </p>

  <h2>Money</h2>
  <p>
    The site currently earns nothing: no affiliate links, no advertising, no sponsored posts,
    no paid placement. If affiliate links are added later they will be disclosed on every page
    that carries them, above the content rather than buried under it, and no recommendation
    will be for sale at any point. The full policy is on the
    <a href={withBase('/affiliate-disclosure/')}>affiliate disclosure page</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
